Hunt for server-side request forgery. A source is any user-controlled URL, host, IP, port, path, or destination identifier (query/body/header/path param, or a value derived from one). A sink is any place the server uses that destination to make — or is intended to make, or returns for later use — an outbound request: HTTP/S clients, fetch/curl/wget wrappers, webhooks, URL openers, redirect followers, file/protocol fetchers, image/PDF/SSR renderers, OIDC/metadata lookups, or proxy passthroughs. Report a finding when a user-controlled destination reaches such a sink WITHOUT robust validation. Treat the following as vulnerable, even with no obvious outbound client call present: missing validation; weak or bypassable allowlists/denylists (e.g. startswith/substring/host-prefix checks an attacker can satisfy yet still reach internal targets); scheme or host confusion (file://, gopher://, embedded credentials, alternate encodings); open redirects the server follows; and any path that exposes or forwards cloud metadata or internal-only endpoints (link-local 169.254.x.x, loopback, RFC1918 ranges). The presence of a hardcoded outbound HTTP call is NOT required — accepting and trusting a user-controlled destination is the core issue.
